One detail changes how you should read any AI control report, including ours.

In the Section 5 lifecycle clauses of the MAS Guidelines, most expectations are not "does the artefact exist" but "is the artefact adequate". So in our catalogue such a control is labelled semi: a machine confirms the artefact exists and is current; a human judges adequacy.

A semi control therefore never reports PASS. When its automated check succeeds, the outcome is NEEDS_ATTESTATION.

On the fictional portfolio that means {{portfolio.needs_attestation}} checks need attestation and {{portfolio.fail}} are genuine automated failures. Read the first number as failures and you will panic; read it as a work queue and it is the honest answer.

Counted per control, semi is the largest class ({{rq1.controls_semi_share}}), ahead of auto ({{rq1.controls_auto_share}}) and doc ({{rq1.controls_doc_share}}). And {{rq5.semi_doc_effort_share}} of estimated compliance effort sits in the semi and doc classes - the part no tool finishes for you.

Section 5 binds from 7 Oct 2028. The work is mostly people.

